WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.339
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.090
SPEAKER 1: Data
centers use about 1%

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:13.590
of electricity in the world.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.640
So today, let's explore how
data centers are powered,

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.072
especially in a
world confronting

00:00:18.072 --> 00:00:19.280
the threat of climate change.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:26.320
In September 2020,
Google CEO Sundar Pichai

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.180
unveiled that
Google would become

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.340
one of the first
companies to operate

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:37.090
carbon-free 24 hours a day, 7
days a week, 365 days a year

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:39.910
by 2030.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.130
Carbon-free means
running data centers

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:46.390
on clean electricity that does
not come from fossil fuels.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:48.820
But how does a global
technology company

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.100
with a constant
need for electricity

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.340
achieve the ability to slash
all of its carbon emissions

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.310
within a decade?

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.770
Well, that's a big question.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:01.250
And to answer it, we
have to go back in time.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:05.360
In 2006, Google installed
over 9,000 solar panels

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.120
in its Mountain View,
California buildings,

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.030
and learned how to optimize
our efficiency with tricks

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:15.180
like finding optimal
locations and washing them.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.010
But six years
later, it was still

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:20.580
a unique and custom process
to buy renewable energy.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.370
Traditional power companies
didn't have the experience

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:27.120
with the volume of wind and
solar that Google was seeking.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.070
But fast-forward to
today, and Google

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.430
has become the largest corporate
purchaser of clean energy.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:36.220
And utility companies now
have an idea of what's needed,

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:39.260
making it an industry standard.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:42.170
Just like computer chips,
higher production volumes

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.440
create lower costs.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.750
As clean energy has
gone mainstream,

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.220
the cost has declined.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.640
And wind and solar are now
cheaper than any other resource

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.150
in many areas of the world.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:59.140
Adding clean energy to grids is
only one part of the strategy.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:02.710
Data centers also need to
be designed efficiently.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.620
Historically, it took a
massive amount of cooling.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.990
A data center
technician in the 1980s

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.150
would be wearing a
very thick sweater,

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:14.710
since temperatures inside were
around 58 degrees all the time.

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.810
Today, a T-shirt suffices.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.840
A lot of effort has been placed
on designing better cooling

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:25.520
systems and isolating the hot
aisles from the cold aisles.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.910
In fact, in 2018, a
data center engineer

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.600
created an ML algorithm
that exponentially improved

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:36.000
cooling loads by at least 30%.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.090
This AI-powered efficiency
recommendation system

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:41.490
ingested all the
cooling system data

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:45.120
we had on power,
temperature, and pump speeds

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.310
and fed it to
neural networks that

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.400
learned how to operate the
mechanical systems more

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.140
efficiently.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.330
Teams of engineers
also worked on how

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.730
to make the servers more
efficient to squeeze

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:00.030
more computation
out of each machine.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.760
In the past, an idle
server could use just

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:07.500
as much energy as one that is
processing at max capacity.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.650
One way to improve this capacity
was to virtualize machines.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.790
That means you take a physical
device such as a server

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:19.170
and run multiple instances
of an operating system.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.280
It's almost like having 10, 20,
or even 50 different computing

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.830
loads running on
one physical device,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:30.570
keeping utilization high
and energy emissions low.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:34.770
Between 2010 and 2018,
overall computing efficiency

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:39.180
grew by 550% in data centers.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.590
And in 2019, Google reported
that its data centers delivered

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.080
over seven times as
much computing power

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.810
with the same amount
of electrical energy.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:52.120
Google began to buy
enough solar and wind

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:55.330
energy to match its total
electricity use worldwide

00:03:55.330 --> 00:04:00.260
since 2017, but there were
still more work to do.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.530
For example, there
were still data centers

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:05.440
that could only use solar
energy during the day,

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:08.350
but at night were using
carbon-intensive energy

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:09.740
from the grid.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:11.380
So the energy teams
at Google started

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:14.410
thinking about our mission in
a completely different way.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:18.490
How could we match every
bit by search, payment,

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:22.810
and translation, to a
zero-carbon electron?

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.240
Googler Anna
Radovanovich understood

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.580
that there was a way
to use data to tell

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:31.540
when power was clean or
dirty at any given time.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:35.230
Since Google logs data on
every single compute workload,

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:37.420
we could analyze
which tasks did not

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.330
need to happen immediately,
such as the training of machine

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:41.650
learning models.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:44.650
These tasks could be safely
scheduled at a later time

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:48.670
and powered with clean energy
without affecting performance.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.740
This is called demand
response, which

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.980
is not a new concept for big
energy consumers like grocery

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:57.340
stores, offices, or factories
who reduce their energy demand

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:00.490
at certain times of the day.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.540
Even though this concept
has been around a long time,

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:06.510
Google is going about it
in a pretty unique way.

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:08.820
And carbon-aware
computing has made

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.310
a huge shift in the industry.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.310
Although complicated,
it's key to decarbonizing

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:16.770
the digital economy.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:20.310
By mid 2020, all the
elements came together--

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.640
incredibly cheap renewable
energy, radical improvements

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:28.080
in efficiency, higher-density
batteries, green hydrogen,

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:31.020
and novel types of
greener nuclear plants.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:34.200
For the first time, Google
was able to put a firm

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.650
date on our 24/7 carbon goal.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:40.860
This is so important to
us, as the next decade

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.350
is vital to limit
climate change and make

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:47.280
a huge shift in our relationship
with natural resources.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.040
If you want to learn more,
check out the links below,

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:54.230
and stay tuned for more on
discovering data centers.

